<Layout title="how|we|do" description="Curriculum vitae of Howie Seay — product leadership in life-sciences software and flow cytometry">
  <section id="main" class="wrapper">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="major special">
        <h2>CURRICULUM VITAE</h2>
        <p><em>When you come to a fork in the road, take it</em></p>
      </header>

      <span class="image fit"><img src="/images/banner.jpg" alt="" /></span>

      <h2>Professional Experience</h2>

      <h3>Senior Product Manager &mdash; Cellares</h3>
      <p><b>South San Francisco, California &middot; June 2022 &ndash; Present</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Lead product across four scrum teams and 30+ engineers building the software for the
            Cell Shuttle, a fully automated cell therapy manufacturing platform &mdash;
            orchestration, scheduling, reagent tracking, and information management.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Defined how three spectral analyzers integrate with an automated quality control
            platform running standard cell therapy flow panels, and operated them during
            integration testing.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Specified the requirements for integrating a GMP closed cell sorter into the
            manufacturing process, moving cell selection inside the closed system rather than
            leaving it as a separate open step.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Delivered the traceability and record-keeping that allow the platform to be used for
            real patient doses under FDA-regulated quality systems.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Demonstrate the Cell Shuttle and Cell Q instruments to customers, work the CAR-TCR
            Summit booth, and run structured user research with the analysts who operate them.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Senior Product Owner &mdash; Rosalind Bioinformatics</h3>
      <p><b>San Diego, California &middot; January 2022 &ndash; June 2022</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Led product for a genomics research platform using machine learning to turn sequencing
            data into evidence pharmaceutical and clinical users could act on.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Took a diagnostic monitoring system to launch with NIH RADx Tech support, alongside a
            FedRAMP-authorized platform used under emergency authorization to triage patients by
            diagnosed variant.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Senior Product Owner / Product Manager &mdash; BD Biosciences / FlowJo</h3>
      <p><b>Ashland, Oregon &middot; December 2018 &ndash; January 2022</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Invented and patented a method for multicolor panel design that models and minimizes
            spillover spreading &mdash; granted as US 11,935,629 and assigned to BD, with family
            members in the EU, China, and Japan.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Helped develop and test the spectral unmixing in FlowJo, the analysis software
            cytometrists use to unmix full spectral data.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Gathered requirements for BD Research Cloud face to face with users, application
            scientists, technical support, and marketing &mdash; working the BD booth at CYTO and
            the American Association of Immunologists annual meeting, and visiting customer sites.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Built the business cases, financial analyses, and ROI models BD used to decide which
            software to invest in, including the cloud analysis platform.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Researched licensing structure and pricing with the sales and strategy team, and
            maintained the competitive assessments behind that positioning.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Core Research Facility Manager / Laboratory Manager &mdash; University of Florida</h3>
      <p><b>Gainesville, Florida &middot; February 2011 &ndash; November 2018</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Ran the fee-for-service flow cytometry and cell sorting cores serving many independent
            research groups &mdash; fully responsible for service rates and cost structure,
            returning a quarterly profit of $10,000&ndash;$30,000 while recovering instrument and
            staff cost.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Operated and maintained conventional and full spectral analyzers and cell sorters
            including a Cytek Aurora, and handled the troubleshooting and quality control that kept
            them running.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Created and led training courses in flow cytometry, advanced multicolor panel design,
            and instrument operation, mentoring hundreds of researchers and staff.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Supported cell sorting programs tied to cord blood regulatory T-cell work that drew
            approximately $800,000 in industry sponsorship.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Authored the cGMP protocol for isolating cryopreserved cord-blood regulatory T cells for
            autologous infusion, achieved cGMP certification, and managed IRB and IACUC protocols.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

